WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.600
<v Instructor>Okay, so now let us talk about</v>

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:03.470
Auto Scaling Groups.

3
00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:05.730
And the first kind of policy we're going to see

4
00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.730
is Dynamic Scaling Policies.

5
00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:08.640
So we have three kinds.

6
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.540
We have the Target Tracking Scaling,

7
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.930
and this is by far the most simple and easy to set up

8
00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:16.260
because all you have to say is take a metric.

9
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:19.840
For example, the average ASG CPU and give it a target.

10
00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.140
For example, you want it to stay

11
00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:23.850
at around 40% across your ASG.

12
00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:26.490
Well, in that case, in case the CPU is too high,

13
00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:28.720
then the ASG will add instances

14
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.280
and in case the CPU is too low,

15
00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:33.570
then the ASG will terminate instances for you.

16
00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:35.660
If you wanted to go back in complexity

17
00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:36.560
and add more complexity,

18
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:38.640
so you can set up a CloudWatch alarm,

19
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.480
and for example, that CloudWatch alarm could say,

20
00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.020
hey, if it's triggered because the CPU is more than 70%,

21
00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:47.350
then add to units, this is what's called step scaling.

22
00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:49.150
And if your CloudWatch alarm, for example,

23
00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:51.740
is triggered because the CPU is less than 30%,

24
00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:52.573
then remove your units.

25
00:00:52.573 --> 00:00:53.880
This is again, step scaling.

26
00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:54.960
So it's a bit more involved

27
00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.940
because you need to set up these rules yourself,

28
00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:59.060
but it gives you more control as well

29
00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:02.070
into how the auto scaling group is scaling.

30
00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:03.530
And then finally you have scheduled actions.

31
00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.160
For example, you know that

32
00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:06.810
something is going to happen in advance.

33
00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:09.770
For example, you know that on Fridays at 5:00 PM,

34
00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:11.610
you're going to have a big increase in usage

35
00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:12.630
because I don't know,

36
00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:14.560
you're running a promotion every Friday at 5:00 PM

37
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.860
and so therefore, maybe you want to know that by in advance

38
00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:19.720
you should increase the minimum capacity

39
00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:22.470
to 10 instances to sustain that load.

40
00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:25.050
On the opposite side, you have predictive scaling

41
00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.760
and this is to continuously forecast load

42
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:30.620
and then you are going to schedule scaling ahead.

43
00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:33.850
So AWS will analyze your historical load

44
00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:36.740
and then generate a forecast based on your load

45
00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:40.400
and then set up automatically scaling actions in advance

46
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.200
to predict the right of amount of instances you need

47
00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.800
to sustain that load.

48
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:47.633
So it's going to be good

49
00:01:47.633 --> 00:01:50.720
especially if you have like patterns in your load

50
00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.470
from week to week or from day to day.

51
00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:55.100
Now, good metrics to scale on ASG

52
00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.840
is CPU utilization, Request Count Per Target.

53
00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.890
So to ensure that the number of requests

54
00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:02.960
per EC2 instances is stable and they're not overloaded

55
00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:06.410
in terms of network request per ports,

56
00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:07.760
and so this is how it goes.

57
00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:08.593
For example, you say,

58
00:02:08.593 --> 00:02:11.890
hey, I want three request per target maximum.

59
00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:14.420
So that means that the ASG will try to make sure

60
00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:16.810
that any point of time each EC2 instance

61
00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:18.650
is replying to three requests.

62
00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:20.670
Now in real life, obviously this is more something

63
00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:23.970
like 1000 unit sets, average in and out.

64
00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.730
So for example, if you're application takes a lot of network

65
00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:30.330
in or out, for example, if you upload a download video files

66
00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:32.460
through your EC2 instances, then you know

67
00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.040
that your EC2 instance has a limit

68
00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:36.050
in terms of how much networking it can allow in,

69
00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:37.580
therefore you would use this kind of metric

70
00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:40.440
to scale in and out, or you can have any custom metric

71
00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:41.930
that you post using CloudWatch

72
00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:43.750
and use these metrics to scale on.

73
00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:46.370
Okay, some other things that are good to know for your ASG.

74
00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:48.050
Well, you have spot fleet support.

75
00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:50.750
That means that you can mix, spot and on-demand instances

76
00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:52.370
in your auto scaling group.

77
00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:53.870
You have lifecycle hooks

78
00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:55.290
which allows you to perform action

79
00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:58.540
before an instance in service or before it is terminated.

80
00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:00.440
For example, if you want to do cleanup

81
00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:02.470
of your instance or log extraction,

82
00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:05.680
or special health checks before an instance is in service.

83
00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:07.500
And finally, if you want to avoid an AMI

84
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:08.970
you have two ways of doing it.

85
00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:11.520
First, obviously you must update the underlying

86
00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:14.170
launch templates or launch configuration of your ASG,

87
00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:15.440
but then you have two ways of doing it.

88
00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:16.980
So either you go ahead and you terminate

89
00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:18.790
EC2 instances manually,

90
00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:22.730
and confirmation can help to automate that rolling upgrade.

91
00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:25.100
Or you would use something called EC2 instance refresh

92
00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:28.010
for auto scaling, which will terminate instances for you.

93
00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:30.560
What happens that when you terminate instances in an ASG

94
00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:31.910
then new ones come up

95
00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:34.950
and they will have the new AMI by default, because well,

96
00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:37.470
your ASG is configured to use the new launch templates

97
00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:39.110
or launch configuration.

98
00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:40.410
So talking about this feature,

99
00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:41.870
Instance Refresh, let's have a look.

100
00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:44.690
So the goal is to recreate all EC2 instances

101
00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:48.070
from the latest launch templates in your ASG.

102
00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:49.390
And so there's our feature.

103
00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:51.460
So if you have an ASG, for example, like this

104
00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:53.980
that has a couple or many EC2 instances

105
00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:55.870
using the old launch templates,

106
00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:58.720
then we're going to upload a new launch templates

107
00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:01.160
with an updated AMI, for example, in our ASG.

108
00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:04.030
And then we'll use the API start instance refresh.

109
00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:06.840
We're going to specify a minimum healthy percentage

110
00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.230
of instances so that not all instances

111
00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:10.160
are destroyed at once.

112
00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:12.650
So for example, we can say, hey, I want 60%

113
00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:15.020
of my instances to be healthy at any point of time.

114
00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:18.780
And then the ASG natively, is going to terminate an instance

115
00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:21.650
and then create a new one using the new launch templates,

116
00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:25.470
and so on until all your instances have been upgraded

117
00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:27.540
to the new launch templates.

118
00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.320
We can also specify a warmup time,

119
00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.600
which is how long until the instances are ready to use

120
00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:34.993
to go and iterate over terminating the old instances.

121
00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:37.840
Now let's talk about all the processes

122
00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:39.880
that are within your auto scaling group.

123
00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:41.230
The first one is a launch process

124
00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:43.210
which will add EC2 instances to the group,

125
00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:44.660
increasing the capacity.

126
00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:46.293
The terminate process that removes

127
00:04:46.293 --> 00:04:47.570
an EC2 instance from the group,

128
00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:49.410
therefore decreasing its capacity.

129
00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:51.400
The health check, the process that's checks the health

130
00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:54.110
of your instances, replace unhealthy,

131
00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:56.940
which is a process that will terminate unhealthy instances

132
00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:58.530
and recreate them.

133
00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:01.270
AZ rebalance which is a process that looks at your ASG

134
00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:03.870
and making sure that there is an equal or balanced number

135
00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:07.330
of EC2 instances across all your ability zones.

136
00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:10.730
Alarm notification which is a process that will accept

137
00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:13.480
notifications for CloudWatch in terms of scaling.

138
00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:16.010
Scheduled actions which is a process that will perform

139
00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:19.093
the scheduled actions that you create for your scaling.

140
00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:21.390
Then you have add to load balancer,

141
00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:23.660
which is a process that will add instances

142
00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:26.440
from the ASG to your desired load balancer

143
00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:29.080
or the target group of that load balancer.

144
00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:31.120
And then instance refresh that we just saw,

145
00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:33.370
which is a process that performs an instance refresh

146
00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:36.770
in the ASG by terminating all the instances

147
00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:38.370
and recreating new one.

148
00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:39.970
And the cool thing about ASG is that,

149
00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:42.280
all these processes can be suspended.

150
00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:43.990
So if you do not want, for example

151
00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:45.780
to replace the unhealthy instances

152
00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:47.670
because you wanna troubleshoot them for a bit,

153
00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:50.680
then you can suspend the replaced unhealthy process

154
00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:53.973
and debug while you find an instance to be unhealthy.

155
00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:57.220
Next for health checks, extremely important.

156
00:05:57.220 --> 00:06:00.820
Your ASG depends on health checks to function correctly

157
00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:03.160
and you have different health checks available.

158
00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:05.370
The first one is the EC2 status checks, health check,

159
00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:06.730
and we've seen this from before.

160
00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:09.100
And the second one, if you have an ELB

161
00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:11.330
in front of your auto scaling group,

162
00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:15.120
then it's an ELB health check and it's HTTP based.

163
00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:19.290
The idea is that if your instances is deemed unhealthy

164
00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:21.390
because it has a bad EC2 status check

165
00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:24.100
or has a bad ELB health check,

166
00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:27.030
then your ASG will launch a new instance

167
00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:29.320
after terminating an unhealthy one.

168
00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:31.540
So the idea is that if you have a bad health check

169
00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:33.410
then the ASG will go crazy

170
00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:35.520
and start terminating in all your instances.

171
00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:36.730
So you need to make sure that the health check

172
00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:37.830
is going to you be simple

173
00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:39.520
and they will check the correct thing,

174
00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:42.500
which is the health of the instance.

175
00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:44.260
So let's make an example.

176
00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:47.000
So a good health check is when an auto scaling group

177
00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:49.770
for example, checks a route on your EC2 instance

178
00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:51.740
called slash health server

179
00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:53.030
and the server that says,

180
00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:54.870
yes I'm alive and I'm functioning correctly.

181
00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:56.420
This is a good health check.

182
00:06:56.420 --> 00:06:58.860
A bad health check is where an auto scaling group

183
00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:01.740
will check a route that does something quite heavy.

184
00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:04.890
For example, if we check the slash number of customers route

185
00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:07.710
on our EC2 instance, and our EC2 instance has to do

186
00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:11.050
a database call to count all the number of customers,

187
00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:12.750
wait for the database to respond to us,

188
00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:14.770
and then get the answer to the auto scaling group.

189
00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:16.170
In case the database fails

190
00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:18.210
or in case something takes too long,

191
00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:19.610
then the auto scaling group will think

192
00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:23.030
that your EC2 instance is unhealthy and will terminate it.

193
00:07:23.030 --> 00:07:24.910
So making sure you have the right health check

194
00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:27.830
is extremely important in your solution architecture.

195
00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:29.350
Okay, so that's it for small reminders

196
00:07:29.350 --> 00:07:30.390
on auto scaling group.

197
00:07:30.390 --> 00:07:31.730
In the next lecture we'll see

198
00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:33.790
how we can properly update an auto scaling group,

199
00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:36.050
and what are the different solution architectures around it.

200
00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:37.600
So see you in the next lecture.

